user is authenticated, forcing a redundant re-authentication. I\'m now exploring solutions to store the `cachedAccessToken`. I am seriously considering using `sessionStorage` or `localStorage` to retain the access token, to resolve the inconvenience and improve the user experience. I am currently confirming if this is safe.\n\n\n**Implementing Token Persistence**\n\nI\'m now implementing token persistence using `localStorage`. I\'ll store the Google access token under the key `pe_google_access_token`. Upon app load, I\'ll attempt to retrieve the token from `localStorage` within `initAuth`. I\'ll update the `cachedAccessToken` variable if a token is found. If no token is found, or the user logs out, the existing token will be removed, ensuring a clean state. I\'m checking to ensure this doesn\'t create any new security concerns.\n\n\n**Addressing Token Persistence**\n\nI\'ve confirmed that a simple page refresh causes the access token to be lost, requiring re-authentication. I\'m now implementing `localStorage` to persist the token, stored under the key `pe_google_access_token`. This involves retrieving the token from storage during app initialization within `initAuth` and updating `cachedAccessToken`. I also plan to clear the token from storage upon user logout. I must be very careful of any security concerns. And, I think I\'ve also found the cause of the 403 error.\n\n\n**Verifying Decimal Inclusion**\n\nI\'m now focused on the implementation of `syncToDrive` and ensuring it includes decimal places within the backup file. My latest realization is that if `fetchAllocations()` returns allocation data without decimal place settings, those settings will be missing in the backup. The backup file format is `JSON.stringify({ assets, allocations })`. I have to confirm that `fetchAllocations()` accounts for decimal places, and that data is saved correctly to local storage. This will prevent a loss of user settings during backup restoration. I must ensure the load strategie