reamlined approach! I\'ve confirmed that a simple two-step process is indeed the solution. This is great, and so easy. We use a POST for the metadata. Then we do a PATCH to update the file content with uploadType=media. This eliminates multipart complexity. The Google Drive v3 API fully supports file content updates via PATCH requests using uploadType=media. This removes the need for `FormData` or multipart boundaries. I\'m now certain this avoids potential body parsing problems.\n\n\n**Debugging Sync Failure**\n\nI\'m now focusing on a direct error inspection within `syncToDrive` to understand and debug the behavior. I\'m going to look at what occurs, including the status code, in order to diagnose the root issue.\n\n\n**Debugging Sync Implementation**\n\nI\'m now focusing on implementing the two-step upload process directly in `syncToDrive`. I\'m starting with the initial POST request to create the file metadata, then integrating the PATCH request to update the file content. I\'m focusing on ensuring the correct authorization header and request body format for each step. My immediate goal is a working, reliable sync function.\n\n\n**Implementing Metadata Creation**\n\nI\'m now implementing the first step in `syncToDrive`: creating file metadata with a `POST` request. I\'m carefully constructing the JSON body with the filename and parent folder details. My immediate focus is on handling potential errors from this initial request.\n\n\n**Enhancing Error Reporting**\n\nI\'m now focusing on improving error handling within the `getDriveFileId` function. It previously only returned `null` upon failure. I\'m adding comprehensive error logging to capture status codes and response details, which will greatly improve debugging. I want to have enough detail in case of failure.\n\n\n**Examining Sync Download**\n\nI\'m now shifting gears to `syncFromDrive`, examining the file download process. I\'m focusing on the `getDriveFileId` function, and how it retrieves the file ID. Ne