oogle API using the stored access token return 401/403 errors, despite the user\'s logged-in Firebase state. This causes sync operations to fail. The user currently has to manually disconnect and reconnect. My updated strategy is to proactively detect 401/403 errors within `syncToDrive` and `syncFromDrive`. Upon detecting these errors, I will automatically trigger `googleSignIn` in an attempt to get a fresh token, to create a smarter solution.\n\n\n**Refining Error Handling**\n\nI\'m now refining error handling. Instead of a generic error, I\'ll throw a specific `\'UNAUTHORIZED_TOKEN\'` from within Google API calls, specifically if `response.status` is 401 or 403, and then catch this error elsewhere to trigger a fresh token request automatically using `googleSignIn`. This will occur in `syncToDrive` and `syncFromDrive`.\n\n\n**Validating User Interaction**\n\nI\'m now focusing on a robust way to handle token expiry. My latest thought is that if a 401/403 error, which signals the token is expired, occurs within the context of a user action, like clicking \"Backup\" or \"Restore\", then I can safely trigger a `googleSignIn()` immediately. This should refresh the token without the browser blocking the popup. This will need to be carefully validated in edge cases. This approach will maximize ease of use.\n\n\n"
